AB  - Methyldopa, a centrally-acting antihypertensive agent and first-line therapy for gestational hypertension, requires sensitive and selective analytical methods for therapeutic drug monitoring. This work presents a novel fluorometric sensor based on phenylboronic acid-functionalized carbon dots (PB@CDs) for methyldopa quantification in biological fluids. The sensing mechanism exploits reversible covalent boronate ester formation between phenylboronic acid moieties and methyldopa's catechol group, triggering aggregation-induced fluorescence quenching through hydrogen bonding interactions. PB@CDs were synthesized via solvothermal carbonization of polyethyleneimine and glyoxal, followed by surface modification with 4-(bromomethyl)phenylboronic acid. The aggregation-induced quenching mechanism was validated by DLS, TEM, zeta potential changes, and fluorescence lifetime reduction. Under optimized conditions, the method exhibited excellent linearity (0.1–150.0 μM, R2 = 0.9996) with a limit of detection of 0.059 μM. Exceptional selectivity was demonstrated against potential interferents at physiological concentrations, including structurally related compounds and co-administered medications. Application to spiked plasma samples yielded recovery values of 98.0 % - 100.1 % with RSD <3.7 %. Successful pharmacokinetic profiling in healthy volunteers receiving methyldopa demonstrated the method's capability to cover both peak and terminal elimination concentrations, establishing its suitability for therapeutic drug monitoring and clinical applications.
ER  - 

