AB  - Ticagrelor and its pharmacologically active metabolite AR-C124910XX exert equipotent P2Y12 receptor inhibition, rendering simultaneous plasma quantification of both compounds essential for accurate therapeutic drug monitoring and pharmacokinetic assessment. This study describes the development, optimization, and full ICH M10-compliant validation of an ultrasound-assisted dispersive liquid-liquid microextraction coupled LC-MS/MS method for simultaneous quantification of both analytes in human plasma. Extraction conditions were optimized through a univariate one-factor-at-a-time approach across six parameters — extraction solvent type and volume, disperser solvent type and volume, sample pH, and ultrasonic irradiation time. Ethyl acetate (250 μL) and acetonitrile (500 μL) were selected as optimal extraction and disperser solvents, respectively. Sample pH was adjusted to 5.0 using acetate buffer to maximize disruption of plasma protein binding exceeding 99.8% through albumin conformational changes, and 3 min ultrasonic irradiation achieved maximum cavitation-enhanced mass transfer. Both analytes were detected in negative electrospray ionization mode, with ticagrelor and AR-C124910XX sharing a common product ion at m/z 361.10 that necessitated chromatographic baseline resolution on a Poroshell 120 EC-C18 column within a 10 min run time. Tolbutamide, monitored at m/z 269.00 → 169.60, served as internal standard, with AR-C124910XX, ticagrelor, and tolbutamide eluting at 3.17, 4.05, and 2.10 min, respectively. The method demonstrated linearity over 2–1000 ng/mL with r2 exceeding 0.999, LLOQ of 2 ng/mL, extraction recoveries exceeding 92%, matrix effects within 91–106%, and accuracy and precision within ICH M10 acceptance criteria at all quality control levels, with analyte stability confirmed under all relevant conditions. Greenness and practicality assessment using AGSA-Prep, MoGAPI, and BAGI yielded scores of 71, 68, and 75 out of 100, respectively, consistently reflecting moderate-to-good environmental and practical performance of the developed procedure. Application to a single-dose pharmacokinetic study in eight healthy volunteers receiving 90 mg ticagrelor yielded mean peak plasma concentration (Cmax) of 343 ± 82 and 102 ± 28 ng/mL and mean area under the plasma concentration-time curve from time zero to infinity (AUC0→∞) of 4181 ± 1031 and 1561 ± 529 ng·h/mL for ticagrelor and AR-C124910XX, respectively. The AUC-based metabolic ratio (MRAUC) revealed a mean value of 0.377 ± 0.094, enabling non-invasive CYP3A4 phenotyping with identification of one reduced metabolizer, illustrating the potential applicability of this platform for future precision antiplatelet therapy monitoring.
